  "notes": [
   "Parts are unchanged from step 1.",
   "The tutorial does not give the exact wiring, only that each of four buttons goes to its own GPIO pin and (in the standard GPIO button wiring) to GND. I used the example GPIO numbers from the code: 4 (drum), 17 (cymbal), 27 (snare), 10 (bell). gpiozero Button uses the internal pull-up, so no external resistor is needed.",
   "Each button straddles the breadboard centre gap: side 1 (1.l, 1.r) in the top half at columns c and c+2, side 2 (2.l, 2.r) in the bottom half. The GPIO jumper goes to the top (side 1) column and a pin-to-pin jumper takes the bottom (side 2) column to the bottom negative rail (bn).",
   "The fifth pin-to-socket lead runs from a Pi GND pin to bn.1 of the breadboard. The four pin-to-pin leads join each button's side 2 to the rail. bn is treated as one rail strip, so bn.1, bn.2, bn.8, bn.13 and bn.18 are the same node.",
   "Pin-to-socket leads: the female end (port a) goes on the Pi header pin and the male end (port b) goes into the breadboard.",
   "Pi GPIO header pins are named GPIO4, GPIO17, GPIO27, GPIO10 and GND as the task instructs, though the catalogue entry for raspberry-pi-4 lists only GPIO_HEADER.",
   "Headphones or speakers plug into the Pi's 3.5mm audio jack. The catalogue has no port for it, so that edge has a null Pi port and is joined only, with no electrical edge.",
   "The program sets pygame's own audio output and the software steps (sample files, Thonny) are not physical parts, so they are not in the graph."
  ]
 },
